INT. HOSPITAL - CARDIAC CATH LAB - DAY

Bright, cold, sterile. Monitors blink green and amber. A fluoroscope arm hangs over a table like a heron.

DR. IMOGEN ACHEBE-LARSEN (40s, surgical cap, calm like a held breath) scrubs at the sink, eyes on a screen. NURSE FELIX OYAMA (30s, steady hands, mask pushed up on his forehead) lays out instruments in perfect rows.

On the table lies HARLAN VOSKUIJLEN (60s, gown, wrist bands, a farmer's tan line at the collar), awake, sedated, hands folded.

In the viewing window, behind glass: NADIA KARIMOVA (50s, his wife, handbag on her lap, knitting needles motionless).

                    IMOGEN
          Mr. Voskuijlen, how are we doing?

                    HARLAN
          Tell me it's a plumbing job.

                    IMOGEN
          It's a plumbing job.

                    HARLAN
          Good. I fixed a tractor once.

                    FELIX
          Little pinch, sir.

Felix slides in the needle. Harlan doesn't wince. He looks at the ceiling, at the light, at a smear of dried tape on a tile.

                    HARLAN
          You ever notice that tile has a stain?

                    FELIX
          Every day.

                    HARLAN
          Shaped like Ohio.

                    FELIX
          Nobody's ever said Ohio.

                    HARLAN
          Nobody asks.

Imogen watches the screen. The catheter threads upward, a gray worm through a black river. She tilts her head, bites her lip behind the mask.

                    IMOGEN
          Left anterior descending. Ninety percent.

                    FELIX
          Stent?

                    IMOGEN
          Stent.

She takes a gleaming device from the tray. Felix hands over the next one without being asked. They work in silence.

In the window, Nadia's needles begin to click. Quick. Quicker. The knitting is a scarf. It has no shape.

                    HARLAN
          Is she knitting?

                    IMOGEN
          She is.

                    HARLAN
          She always knits when it's bad.

                    IMOGEN
          It's not bad.

                    HARLAN
          Then why's she on row four hundred?

A monitor chirps. Not loud. A shift in rhythm, a stutter. Felix steps closer.

                    FELIX
          Pressure's dropping.

                    IMOGEN
          I see it.

                    FELIX
          Dr. Achebe.

                    IMOGEN
          I see it.

The beeping quickens. The green line jitters into a spike. Felix grabs the paddles and rolls the cart forward; Imogen presses a hand flat on Harlan's chest.

                    IMOGEN (CONT'D)
          Mr. Voskuijlen. Harlan. Stay with me.

                    HARLAN
          (eyes closing)
          Tell her to finish the scarf.

                    FELIX
          Clear!

The body jerks. The line flatlines for one second, two, and then, jagged, a heartbeat resumes. Then another. Then a rhythm.

Imogen exhales, long. Her shoulders drop an inch. Felix lowers the paddles, blinking.

                    FELIX (CONT'D)
          He's back.

                    IMOGEN
          He never left.

                    FELIX
          You said that very calmly.

                    IMOGEN
          I'm calm when I'm terrified. It's a flaw.

Behind the glass, Nadia has stopped knitting. Her hand is flat on the window, fingers spread.

Imogen looks up. Meets her eyes. Nods once.

Nadia sits back down. Picks up the needles. Starts a new row.

                    HARLAN
          (groggy)
          Ohio.

                    FELIX
          Rest, sir.

                    HARLAN
          It's Ohio. Tell them.

                    FELIX
          I'll tell them.
